The three next states are very close together: one step moves the car only slightly. So the choice depends on small differences in the approximation, which is why a smooth but slightly wrong approximation can flip actions.

### Least squares by hand

Fit $U_\theta(s) = \theta_0 + \theta_1 s$ to $(0, 1)$, $(1, 3)$ and $(2, 4)$. From @eq-least-squares, $\theta = (7/6, 3/2) \approx (1.17, 1.5)$. The predictions are $1.17, 2.67, 4.17$, and the errors $-0.17, +0.33, -0.17$ sum to zero, as they must when a constant feature is included.

